Let \(T = \lim_{x \to 1} \left( \frac{f(x+2)}{f(3)} \right)^{\frac{18}{(x-1)^2}}\) ; This is of the \(1^{\infty}\) form.
\Rightarrow T = e^{\lim_{x \to 1} \frac{18}{(x-1)^2} \left( \frac{f(x+2)}{f(3)} - 1 \right)}
\Rightarrow T = e^{\lim_{x \to 1} \frac{18}{(x-1)^2} \left( \frac{f(x+2)-f(3)}{f(3)} \right)}
Given \(f(3) = 18\).
\Rightarrow T = e^{\lim_{x \to 1} \frac{18}{(x-1)^2} \left( \frac{f(x+2)-f(3)}{18} \right)}
\Rightarrow T = e^{\lim_{x \to 1} \frac{f(x+2)-f(3)}{(x-1)^2}}
The limit in the exponent is of the \(\frac{0}{0}\) form (since \(f(3)=18\) and \(f'(3)=0\)). Apply L'Hopital's Rule:
\Rightarrow T = e^{\lim_{x \to 1} \frac{f'(x+2) \cdot 1}{2(x-1) \cdot 1}}
This is again of the \(\frac{0}{0}\) form (since \(f'(3)=0\)). Apply L'Hopital's Rule again:
\Rightarrow T = e^{\lim_{x \to 1} \frac{f''(x+2) \cdot 1}{2 \cdot 1}}
Substitute \(x=1\) and the given value \(f''(3) = 4\):
\Rightarrow T = e^{\frac{f''(1+2)}{2}} = e^{\frac{f''(3)}{2}} = e^{\frac{4}{2}} = e^2
The problem asks for the value of \(\log_{e} (T)\) (interpreting the problem statement in line with the solution and options).
\Rightarrow \log_{e} (T) = \log_{e} (e^2) = 2

